---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";

import ProblemFramingDiagram from "../../../../assets/courses/eng-design-fab/img/problem-framing-diagram.svg";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/eng-design-fab/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Session 1: Welcome to Digital Manufacturing</h1>
        <p class="text-sn-theme">Unit 2 &mdash; Manufacturing Foundations, Week 1</p>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Unit 2</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Digital Manufacturing</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>For the Next 8 Weeks</h2>
              <p>You're not just students in a shop class.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                You're an engineering team, hired to design and build
                something real for a client.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>The Arc</h2>
              <table class="table table-dark table-sm">
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Weeks</th><th>Phase</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>1&ndash;4</td><td>Manufacturing Foundations</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>5&ndash;8</td><td>The Arcade Cabinet Project</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Manufacturing Foundations (Weeks 1&ndash;4)</h2>
              <ul>
                <li class="fragment">Working with a real client's brief</li>
                <li class="fragment">Materials &amp; manufacturing processes</li>
                <li class="fragment">CAD, leveled up</li>
                <li class="fragment">CAM &mdash; programming a CNC machine</li>
                <li class="fragment">A small practice build, start to finish</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Then: The Real Project</h2>
              <p>
                Weeks 5&ndash;8, you'll get an actual client brief and build a
                genuinely unique arcade cabinet from it &mdash; CNC-cut
                panels, real electronics, your own design.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Everything these first 4 weeks teach you gets spent for real
                there.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today: Working With a Client</h2>
              <p>
                Before any CAD, CAM, or materials &mdash; the skill this
                whole unit starts with.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Session 2 Gave You a Clean Prompt</h2>
              <p>"Build a robot." Vague, but written for a classroom.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Real Clients Don't Write for Engineers</h2>
              <p>
                They describe what they want in their own words &mdash; not
                yours. Translating that is a real, separate skill.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Same 4-Part Framework</h2>
              <Image
                src={ProblemFramingDiagram}
                alt="Diagram showing four boxes — User, Need, Constraints, Success Criteria — with arrows converging down into a single box labeled Problem Statement"
                width={720}
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                The framework from Session 2 doesn't change. What changes is
                how much digging it takes to fill it in.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Practice Client Ask</h2>
              <p>
                "The bus stop by the park doesn't have anywhere to sit, and
                people are stuck waiting in the rain."
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>What's Actually In This Sentence?</h2>
              <p>Read it again. Notice what it doesn't tell you.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>What's Actually In This Sentence?</h2>
              <ul>
                <li class="fragment"><b>User</b> &mdash; "people," but which people? Daily commuters? Elderly riders? Parents with strollers?</li>
                <li class="fragment"><b>Need</b> &mdash; "somewhere to sit" and "stuck in the rain" name two problems, but is the real need comfort, safety, or something else?</li>
                <li class="fragment"><b>Constraints</b> &mdash; not mentioned at all. City budget? Sidewalk space? Can't block foot traffic?</li>
                <li class="fragment"><b>Success Criteria</b> &mdash; not stated. Fewer complaints? Higher ridership? A survey?</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>When It's Not Written Down</h2>
              <p>You don't guess. You ask.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>A Bad Clarifying Question</h2>
              <p>"What do you want?"</p>
              <p class="fragment">Too broad &mdash; makes the client do your job for you.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>A Good Clarifying Question</h2>
              <p>"Is this mainly for everyday commuters, or is accessibility for elderly or disabled riders the bigger concern?"</p>
              <p class="fragment">Specific enough that a non-technical person can actually answer it.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">A vague ask isn't a bad ask.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                It's normal &mdash; real clients aren't engineers. Translating
                it is your job, not a flaw in their brief.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">Don't design before you've finished asking.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Jumping to a solution before Constraints and Success Criteria
                are pinned down is the same mistake Session 1 warned about
                &mdash; skipping Design and going straight to Build.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Next Time</h2>
              <p>Session 2: Materials for Subtractive Manufacturing</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Before you can design anything for a client, you need to know
                what you're actually building it out of.
              </p>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            The practice client ask below is unrelated to the real Week 5
            project on purpose &mdash; today is about practicing the skill,
            not pre-solving the actual brief you'll get later.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 1: Read and Draft (Individually)</h3>
          <p>Practice client ask:</p>
          <blockquote class="border-start border-primary ps-3">
            "The bus stop by the park doesn't have anywhere to sit, and
            people are stuck waiting in the rain."
          </blockquote>
          <p>
            Draft your own read of all four parts. For each one, mark
            whether it's <b class="text-sn-theme">explicit</b> (actually
            stated), <b class="text-sn-theme">implied</b> (a reasonable
            guess), or <b class="text-sn-theme">missing entirely</b>.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>User &mdash; who, specifically?</li>
            <li>Need &mdash; what problem, not what solution?</li>
            <li>Constraints &mdash; budget, space, timeline, anything else?</li>
            <li>Success Criteria &mdash; how would you actually measure it?</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 2: Compare With a Partner</h3>
          <p>
            Trade drafts. Where did you interpret the ask differently? Any
            disagreement here is exactly the kind of ambiguity a clarifying
            question would resolve &mdash; it's not a sign either of you read
            it wrong.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 3: Write 3 Clarifying Questions</h3>
          <p>
            Write 3 questions you'd actually ask this client before starting
            design work. Each one should be specific enough that a
            non-technical person could answer it &mdash; not "what do you
            want?"
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 4: Role-Play (With Your Partner)</h3>
          <p>
            One of you plays the client &mdash; answer only in plain,
            non-technical language, improvising reasonable answers. The other
            asks their 3 clarifying questions live, then revises their 4-part
            breakdown based on the answers. Switch roles if time allows.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Before you move on, check your work against these two questions.</p>

          <div class="text-start">
            <dl>
              <dt>Did you separate stated fact from assumption?</dt>
              <dd>
                Your draft should clearly mark what the client actually said
                versus what you filled in yourself.
              </dd>
              <dt>Are your clarifying questions actually answerable?</dt>
              <dd>
                A non-technical client should be able to answer each one
                without needing to think like an engineer first.
              </dd>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these on your own. Reflections are individual, even when the rest of the session was team work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>Was there a moment you started imagining a solution before you'd finished clarifying the actual need? What caught it, if anything?</li>
            <li>Session 2 is about what a cabinet is actually made of. Before you know anything about the materials &mdash; name one physical property you'd want a cabinet panel to have, and say why.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Submit your initial draft, your 3 clarifying questions, your
            revised breakdown from the role-play, and your reflection.
          </p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
